{"causes":["The Zhou Dynasty's collapse and the subsequent feudal period created an environment where Confucianism emerged as a response to social disorder and the need for moral guidance.","The influence of Taoism and other pre-Confucian philosophies laid the groundwork for Confucius' ideas, which emphasized personal morality and governance over individual spiritual pursuits.","Chinese statecraft during the Spring and Autumn period (771–476 BCE), where local warlords vied for power, necessitated the development of Confucian principles to maintain social order and legitimacy."],"effects":["The spread of Buddhism from India to China in the 1st century CE led to a decline in Confucianism's popularity, but its influence persisted in East Asian thought and governance.","The Mongol conquests (1206–1368 CE) introduced Confucian ideas to Central Asia and Eastern Europe, expanding their reach beyond East Asia.","Confucianism's emphasis on respect for authority and social hierarchy has shaped the modern East Asian concept of 'guanxi' or personal relationships in business and politics."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Qufu","sourceName":"Qufu","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Confucius","targetName":"Confucius","context":"Birthplace of Confucius, a key figure in the development of Confucianism."},{"sourceSlug":"Zhou Dynasty","sourceName":"Zhou Dynasty","verb":"CAUSES","targetSlug":"Confucianism","targetName":"Confucianism","context":"The collapse of the Zhou Dynasty created an environment where Confucianism emerged as a response to social disorder and the need for moral guidance."},{"sourceSlug":"Taoism","sourceName":"Taoism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Confucius","targetName":"Confucius","context":"Pre-Confucian philosophies, such as Taoism, laid the groundwork for Confucius' ideas, which emphasized personal morality and governance over individual spiritual pursuits."},{"sourceSlug":"Buddhism","sourceName":"Buddhism","verb":"TRANSMITS","targetSlug":"Confucianism","targetName":"Confucianism","context":"The spread of Buddhism from India to China in the 1st century CE led to a decline in Confucianism's popularity, but its influence persisted in East Asian thought and governance."},{"sourceSlug":"Mongols","sourceName":"Mongols","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Confucianism","targetName":"Confucianism","context":"The Mongol conquests introduced Confucian ideas to Central Asia and Eastern Europe, expanding their reach beyond East Asia."},{"sourceSlug":"Mongols","sourceName":"Mongols","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Confucianism","targetName":"Confucianism","context":"The Mongol conquests expanded the reach of Confucian ideas beyond East Asia, influencing governance and thought in Central Asia and Eastern Europe."},{"sourceSlug":"Buddhist Monasteries","sourceName":"Buddhist Monasteries","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Confucianism","targetName":"Confucianism","context":"The spread of Buddhism from India to China in the 1st century CE led to a decline in Confucianism's popularity, as Buddhist monasteries became centers of learning and spiritual influence."},{"sourceSlug":"Han Dynasty","sourceName":"Han Dynasty","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"Confucianism","targetName":"Confucianism","context":"During the Han Dynasty, Confucian ideas were transmitted to the West through trade and cultural exchange, influencing Western thought and governance."},{"sourceSlug":"Western Scholars","sourceName":"Western Scholars","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Confucianism","targetName":"Confucianism","context":"During the Renaissance and Enlightenment periods, Western scholars studied and adopted Confucian ideas, recognizing their value in governance, ethics, and personal development."},{"sourceSlug":"Qufu","sourceName":"Qufu","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Confucius' hometown","targetName":"Confucius' hometown","context":"The ancient town of Qufu, where Confucius was born and taught, is considered a sacred site in Chinese culture."},{"sourceSlug":"Zhou Dynasty","sourceName":"Zhou Dynasty","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Confucianism","targetName":"Confucianism","context":"The Zhou Dynasty's emphasis on personal relationships and social hierarchy laid the groundwork for Confucianism's concept of guanxi, or personal relationships in business and politics."}],"places":[{"name":"Qufu","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Zhou Dynasty Capital","role":"Capital City"},{"name":"Lu State","role":"Ruler's Realm"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Confucianism","type":"Reference article","slug":"confucianism"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q9581","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confucianism"],"tags":["culture","Economic Theories & Systems","economic-theory"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Confucianism is remembered as a landmark idea of the Classical era, referenced in 154 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Influenced intellectual traditions and cultural development.","summary":"Confucianism is the philosophical, ethical, and social tradition originating with the teachings of Confucius (Kong Qiu, 551–479 BCE) and developed by his followers over the subsequent millennia — one of the most influential intellectual and cultural systems in world history, which has shaped Chinese, Korean, Japanese, Vietnamese, and broader East Asian civilisation for over 2,500 years. Confucius was a Chinese philosopher, educator, and political adviser of the Spring and Autumn period who developed a system of thought centred on the cultivation of virtue, the importance of ritual and social propriety (li), humaneness or benevolence (ren), the rectification of names (zhengming), and the hierarchical relationships — between ruler and subject, parent and child, husband and wife, elder and younger brother, and friend and friend — that he believed were the foundation of harmonious society.

Confucianism's development from the teachings of Confucius and his immediate disciples (compiled primarily in the Analects) into a state ideology and a comprehensive civilisational framework was a multi-century process. The philosopher Mencius (Mengzi, c. 372–289 BCE) developed Confucius's ethical ideas into a systematic moral philosophy emphasising the innate goodness of human nature, while Xunzi (c. 310–235 BCE) offered a contrasting vision emphasising the importance of ritual and education in shaping moral character. The Han dynasty's (206 BCE–220 CE) adoption of Confucianism as the official state philosophy — and the establishment of the imperial examination system based on mastery of Confucian classics — transformed Confucianism from a philosophical school into the governing ideology of the Chinese empire, a status it would maintain (with modifications and competitions from Buddhism and Daoism) for two millennia.

Neo-Confucianism — the synthesis of Confucian ethics with Buddhist and Daoist metaphysics developed by Song dynasty philosophers (c. 960–1279 CE), particularly Zhu Xi (1130–1200 CE) — represented a fundamental restatement of the tradition that became the dominant form of Confucian orthodoxy in China, Korea, and Japan until the 19th century. The encounter with Western modernity and its challenge to Confucian social norms — particularly in the May Fourth Movement (1919) in China — produced both anti-Confucian iconoclasm and subsequent Neo-Confucian revival movements seeking to reconcile traditional values with modern democratic and scientific thought.","subjects":["Philosophy","East Asian Civilisation","Classical Era","Chinese Philosophy","Intellectual History","Ancient China","Social Ethics","Chinese Thought"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","WORLD_SYSTEMS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Confucianism's ideas on personal morality, governance, and social hierarchy have shaped the modern East Asian concept of 'guanxi' or personal relationships in business and politics. Its influence can be seen in the development of Confucian classics like Mencius, Xunzi, and Zhu Xi.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
